at-is-the-correct-way-to-organize-a-fridge">What is the correct way to organize a fridge?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#where-should-raw-meat-go-in-the-fridge">Where should raw meat go in the fridge?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#should-eggs-go-in-the-fridge-door">Should eggs go in the fridge door?</a></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#what-temperature-should-a-fridge-be">What temperature should a fridge be?</a></li></ul></li><li><a class="kb-table-of-contents__entry" href="#final-thoughts">Final Thoughts</a></li></ul></div></nav>


<h2 class="wp-block-heading">The Fridge Zone Map: Where Everything Goes</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Every fridge has zones. Some are colder, some are warmer, and some should never hold certain foods. Once you know them, organizing your fridge is mostly about putting things back in the right zone.</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Top shelf:</strong> ready-to-eat food like leftovers, drinks, herbs and an eat-first bin.</li>



<li><strong>Middle shelves:</strong> dairy, deli items and eggs in their original carton.</li>



<li><strong>Bottom shelf:</strong> raw meat, poultry and seafood, sealed and in a tray, so nothing can drip onto food below or beside it.</li>



<li><strong>Crisper drawers:</strong> vegetables in one drawer and fruit in the other.</li>



<li><strong>Door:</strong> condiments, sauces, butter and juice. It&#8217;s the warmest part of the fridge, so it&#8217;s not the place for eggs.</li>
</ul>



